    "updated_at": "2015-10-09T08:08:12Z",
    "body": "From what I understand of the spec, it should be basically equivalent to nest `setTimeout` as to use `setInterval`, but it's not clear it is implemented like that in all browsers. Check the following test:\n\n```\ndata:text/html,<script>onclick = function() { var last = new Date(); var times = []; var i = 0; var interval = setInterval(function() { var now = new Date(); times.push(now - last); last = now; i++; if (i == 20) { clearInterval(interval); document.body.innerHTML = times.join(','); } }, 0); }; </script>Click to run test\n```\n\nIn WebKit I get 2,1,1,2,1,5,5,5,5,6,5,5,4,5,5,5,5,5,6,5 which suggests it either doesn't clamp for the first 5, or maybe it clamps to 1ms for the first 5.\n\nIn Blink I get 1,1,1,1,4,4,5,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4 which suggests it either doesn't clamp for the first 4, or maybe it clamps to 1ms for the first 4.\n\nIn Gecko I get 5,4,4,3,4,5,4,4,4,4,4,3,5,4,4,4,4,4,4,4 which suggests it clamps to 4ms from the start.\n\nIn Presto I get 4,4,4,4,4,5,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,5,4 which suggests it clamps to 4ms from the start.\n\nHaven't tested IE/Edge.\n\nFor comparison, here's the equivalent test for `setTimeout`:\n\n```\ndata:text/html,<script>onclick = function() { var last = new Date(); var times = []; var i = 0; setTimeout(function f() { var now = new Date(); times.push(now - last); last = now; i++; if (i == 20) { document.body.innerHTML = times.join(','); } else { setTimeout(f, 0); } }, 0); }; </script>Click to run test\n```\n\nWebKit: 1,2,1,2,1,5,5,5,6,5,4,5,4,5,4,6,5,4,6,5\n\nBlink: 1,1,2,1,5,5,5,6,5,5,5,5,4,5,5,6,5,5,5,6\n\nGecko: 1,0,0,0,5,6,4,5,5,6,5,5,6,5,6,5,5,6,4,6\n\nPresto: 4,4,5,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,5,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,5\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
